    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">This is the sharpest difference between the two. We tested ByteSIM's {{ providerA.primaryCode }} on a real order and it only knocked off about 5%, despite being advertised elsewhere as 10% off. UPeSIM's {{ providerB.primaryCode }} applies a clean, verified 10% at checkout, no asterisk.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is ByteSIM or UPeSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with ByteSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need a real local number for calls or texts, not just data, in one of its supported countries</li>
        <li>You want data, calls, and hotspot sharing bundled into one purchase</li>
        <li>You're fine uploading a passport photo and a selfie to get that number</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with UPeSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You just want data and would rather not do any identity verification at all</li>
        <li>You want to install the eSIM weeks before your trip without it burning days early</li>
        <li>You want a code that actually gives the full advertised discount</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>ByteSIM's advertised discount doesn't match what we measured.</strong> {{ providerA.primaryCode }} is advertised as 10% off elsewhere, but on our own test order it only saved about 5%, roughly the same cut ByteSIM's site already applies automatically through its ongoing Summer Sale. UPeSIM's {{ providerB.primaryCode }} gave the full 10% with nothing held back.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only UPeSIM's plan can sit installed for weeks without starting.</strong> UPeSIM's validity clock starts when your phone actually connects to the destination network, not when you install the eSIM, so you can set it up at home before you fly. It does go stale after 60 days if never activated, per UPeSIM's own FAQ. ByteSIM's plans are also set up to not bill until you tap activate, per its own support, but its product is built around getting a working number quickly after you land, not sitting dormant for weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>ByteSIM vs UPeSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, ByteSIM or UPeSIM</h3>
      <p>Depends what you're buying. For plain data with no number, UPeSIM's per-GB rates in its pricing table are generally cheaper. But ByteSIM's Spain plan we tested bundled far more data (100GB plus 15GB EU roaming) and a real phone number for about the same $13-ish price as UPeSIM's plain 5GB, so the comparison flips once you need a number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do I need ID verification for either one</h3>
      <p>Only for ByteSIM, and only on its local-number plans (a passport photo and a selfie). ByteSIM's data-only plans don't require it. UPeSIM never requires identity verification for any plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which discount code actually works as advertised</h3>
      <p>UPeSIM's REDDIT10. We tested ByteSIM's TPDBYTE on a real order and it only saved about 5%, not the 10% it's advertised as elsewhere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I install either eSIM before I travel</h3>
      <p>UPeSIM is built for this: its plan doesn't start counting until your phone connects to the destination network, so installing weeks ahead costs you nothing, as long as you activate within 60 days. ByteSIM is more built around activating soon after you land to get a working number quickly.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

